Demi Moore was the woman of honor at this year's Baby2Baby Gala, receiving the Giving Tree Award for her work in advocating for vulnerable children.

The event, presented by Paul Mitchell, took over West Hollywood's Pacific Design Center on Saturday, bringing out a star-studded group of Hollywood mothers in support of the organization . The night raised over $20 million in support of Baby2Baby, which provides basic essential items to children in need. Over the last 15 years, the nonprofit, led by co-CEOs Kelly Sawyer Patricof and Norah Weinstein, has distributed 850 million items &#x2014; including diapers, formula and clothing &#x2014; to children across the country. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories TV Florence Pugh Was "So Nervous" to Release 'East of Eden' and Still Hasn't Read the Reviews: "It Was Too Much With This One" Movies Christopher Abbott Reveals How His 'You Can See Everything' Moment Came to Be

Moore was tapped as this year's headlining honoree, with Andrew Garfield on hand to present her award. During her speech, she asked guests to take a deep breath, saying, &#8220;That breath is the breath of relief Baby2Baby provides when a mother no longer has to choose between dinner and diapers, rent or formula. Your child matters. You matter. You are not alone. That is what community is all about. Baby to baby, mother to mother, soul to soul.&#8221;

Kerry Washington, Keke Palmer, Ciara, Chrissy Teigen, Jessica Alba, Jenna Dewan, Julie Bowen, Katy Perry and Kelly Rowland also took part in the evening's program, following a top-tier dinner with offerings from L.A. hotspots including Carbone, Cheesesteaks By Mat&#x16B;, Found Oyster, Funke, Kato, Kazunori by Sushi Nozawa, Moo's Craft Barbecue, Mr Chow, Saffy's and Sqirl, with late-night bites from Burger She Wrote, Chainsaw, Henry's Secret Ice Cream, Jon & Vinny's, Lei'd Cookies and Leora. Cardi B closed out the event with a performance of many of her hits, accompanied by a 20-piece band. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Also on the guest list: Emma Watson, Eiza Gonz&#xE1;lez, Sofia Richie Grainge and Elliot Grainge, Barbara Palvin and Dylan Sprouse, Paris Hilton, Sof&#xED;a Vergara, Orlando Bloom, Justin Trudeau, Camila Alves McConaughey, Zooey Deschanel, Meghan Trainor, Miranda Kerr and Evan Spiegel, Elsa Hosk, Danny Ramirez, Julianne Hough, Vanessa Bryant, Emma Grede, Moore's daughters Rumer, Scout and Tallulah Willis, Charli D'Amelio, Nara Smith and Lucky Blue Smith, Molly Sims and Scott Stuber, Erin Foster, Jay Ellis, Rachel Zoe and Mookie Betts.

As Avengers: Doomsday assembles into theaters next month, Kevin Feige is celebrating more than 25 years of Marvel &#8216;s cinematic journey.

The Marvel Studios president, who was also an associate producer on 2000's X-Men , recently explained that it &#8220;just felt right&#8221; to bring the mutant superherhoes of Xavier's School for Gifted Youngsters into the MCU with Avengers: Doomsday, which premieres Dec. 18 in theaters.

&#8220;You see interviews of Hugh Jackman 15, 20 years ago saying, &#8216;People say it's impossible, but maybe someday the X-Men and the Avengers can all get together.' It just felt right,&#8221; explained Feige to Empire . &#8220;It just felt like the fun way to culminate a quarter-century of storytelling.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Directed by the Russo brothers, Avengers: Doomsday sees the multiverse collapse, forcing heroes from multiple Earths to unite against Doctor Doom (Robert Downey Jr), including the stars of the original live-action X-Men franchise. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

The late Patrick Stewart (Professor X), Ian McKellen (Magneto), Rebecca Romijn (Mystique), James Marsden (Cyclops), Alan Cumming (Nightcrawler) and Kelsey Grammer (Beast) all signed on to reprise their roles in Avengers: Doomsday last year.

Meanwhile, Christopher Abbott (Charles Xavier/Professor X), Adam Driver (Nathaniel Millbury/Mister Sinister) Inde Navarrette (Rogue), Maya Boyd (Storm), Kit Connor (Cyclops), Sadie Sink (Jean Grey), Samara Weaving (Emma Frost) and Asa Germann (Angel) are taking over their roles for the MCU's new generation of mutants in Jake Schreier's X-Men , which is scheduled to premiere on May 5, 2028.

Silvio Bertolini lives in Montevideo, Uruguay, and has just retired after 40 years of work. His reward? A pregnant daughter in Barcelona, a wife hoping for a family trip, and a government that insists he is &#x2013; well, there is no way to put it kindly &#x2013; dead. Declared dead, Silvio tries to fight a bureaucracy with all sorts of tentacles in hopes of getting the error fixed and reclaiming his life and, maybe, his pension.

Some readers may feel this sounds like a nightmare worthy of Kafka. Others may simply feel reminded of their last visit to a government office. Either way, Silvio's story and misadventures are now coming to the big screen. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies 'Fatherland' Wins Top Camerimage Prize as 'Wild Horse Nine' Nabs Audience Award Lifestyle L.A.'s Most Celebrated French Chef Is Staging a Comeback

When I Existed ( Cuando yo exist&#xED;a ) is the title of the social satire that is the feature debut by writers-directors Alejandro Damiani and Martin Avdolov, a Uruguay and Spain co-production fueled by black humor &#x2013; and frustration. The movie will get its world premiere in the Critics' Picks Competition of the 30th edition of the Tallinn Black Nights Film Festival ( P&#xD6;FF ) in Estonia on Wednesday, Nov. 18.

C&#xE9;sar Troncoso ( Another Story of the World ), one of Uruguay's highest-profile stars, leads the film, with Silvia Kutica, N&#xE9;stor Guzzini, Maruja Bustamante, M&#xF3;nica Gonzaga and Mirella Pascual also featuring in the cast.

The cinematographer is Manuel Branaa, with editing handled by Dan Avram, Mart&#xED; Rocca, Damiani and Avdolov. The producer of When I Existed is Federico Cetta for El Cielo Cine. It is a co-production with Spain's Doce Entertainment (David Matamoros) and Uruguay's Metropolis Films (Andr&#xE9;s Rosenblatt), with Moon Ideas (Mat&#xED;as Burgos) as associate producer. The film was made with the support of ACAU , FONA, MVD Filma, MVD Socio, FIC and Ibermedia. m-appeal is handling sales. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;This is a story of reinvention built around the things that truly matter,&#8221; says Cetta. "It's a film born from the deepest fear any human being can have: how are we supposed to live? No one teaches you that," highlights Damiani. And Avdolov shares: &#8220;This story speaks to us in a world where we have the constant feeling that time is slipping away between obligations we don't always choose. What do we do with the time we have? Today, we need small acts of resistance against a logic that tends to make everything increasingly automatic and artificial.&#8221;

THR can now exclusively premiere the trailer for When I Existed . Turn on the English subtitles, put your Kafka glasses on and enjoy!

Avdolov and Damiani talked to THR about the long journey to get the film made, casting a well-known Uruguayan actor as their lead and why people all over the world may recognize the empty offices and multitudes of forms that Silvio encounters on his odyssey through the labyrinth of bureaucracy.

When I Existed may not be as much Kafka as, well, Avdolov puts it this way: &#8220;think maybe it's more post-Kafka. Because nowadays there's nobody in the bureaucracy. There are no people, and we are playing with that concept. We enjoyed making this movie about reality. Now, when you go to some office in some big building, they are all empty, and there are few people working there.&#8221;

The wild goose chase that Silvio embarks on was also somewhat reflected by the filmmakers' persistence that allowed them to get the film completed. How long was the process behind the movie? &#8220;Only five to six years,&#8221; jokes Damiani. Much of that time was spent writing, which started during the COVID pandemic, and especially looking for funding. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;In Uruguay, you have a lot of people doing good things in two jobs,&#8221; he quips. &#8220;For example, Martin and I are not only directors. I am a fitness model.&#8221; And Avdolov chimes in: &#8220;We are soccer players!&#8221;

Well, in fact, Damiani has worked on TV series and film productions as a director, writer, and visual artist, while Avdolov has written short stories, as well as non-fiction and children's books.

Both call their star Troncoso &#8220;amazing.&#8221; And he needed stamina for When I Existed . &#8220;One of the tricky things is that he participates in all the shots, because we maintain the first-person perspective in the movie,&#8221; highlights Damiani. &#8220;So, in the whole movie, he is in all scenes. That's really, really crazy and really, really hard work.&#8221;

Avdolov lauds the star, saying, &#8220;We have to be really thankful about the job [he did] because he made the movie possible.&#8221;

The creative duo also rewrote and reshaped the script for their star. &#8220;When he came on board, we realized that we had an opportunity to have more drama and a better movie, with less comedy,&#8221; Damiani tells THR .

The main pension office building that Silvio keeps having to go to looks and feels like a leviathan. It may not be pretty, but the capital city of Montevideo and the country's past helped provide this stage. &#8220;Uruguay has, like a lot of parts of South America, a lot of brutalist architecture because of things that happened here,&#8221; explains Damiani. &#8220;It was perfect for the film in that sense.&#8221;

Audiences will be able to take messages for their own lives from When I Existed given that Silvio has always focused on his work and career &#x2013; until his retirement money seems out of reach. &#8220;Anyone who tells you they know how to live hasn't learned yet how to live,&#8221; Avdolov tells THR . &#8220;We always have to adapt. It's a really defiant time to live through now because of all the technology, AI and all the wars. But Earth has always been a really hard place to live on. Are we caring for the people in our family and our friends, or are we just living like robots on autopilot? These are some of the essential questions for us.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

And that also makes the issues and challenges that When I Existed explores universal. &#8220;We come from Uruguay, but these are universal themes. Everybody is struggling with these problems and with a fear of the future. It can be a very shallow life but I think we are deeper as human beings. The system makes you live in the shallow [waters] all the time &#x2013; it's about entertainment, entertainment, dopamine, entertainment, dopamine, entertainment, and you don't have time to read a book. So take time for yourself!&#8221;

Asked about their creative influences, Damiani shares that &#8220;one of my favorite movies is Brazil from Terry Gilliam, but we wanted a new point of view.&#8221; He describes that perspective this way: &#8220;We cannot beat the system, we cannot beat the bureaucracy. But that is not the important thing. The important thing is that we are free to realize that we can [re-evaluate] our own lives.&#8221;

It's been a triumphant year for movie theater owners, who are basking in the long-awaited box office rebound. Domestic ticket sales are already 20% above the same point in 2025, and that's before a &#8220;Hunger Games&#8221; prequel, a third &#8220;Dune&#8221; and another &#8220;Avengers&#8221; installment hit the big screen.

With revenues at $8.76 billion through Nov. 15, according to Rentrak, the industry is poised to exceed $10 billion in North America for the first time since the pandemic upended the theatrical business. Prior to COVID, yearly ticket sales were regularly nearing or surpassing the $11 million mark. In the past half-decade, though, revenues have stalled below $9 billion, with receipts tapping out at $8.87 billion in 2025 and $8.7 billion in 2024 and $8.91 billion in 2023. This year, returns eclipsed $8 billion by October, a milestone the industry didn't reach until December last year. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Priyanka Chopra Jonas, Russell Crowe Sci-Fi Thriller 'Bluefly' Adds 'Beast' Star Daniel MacPherson (EXCLUSIVE)

Despite the popularity of premium large formats like Imax, which have higher prices, attendance has yet to return to pre-pandemic levels. North American revenues remain 9% behind 2019, the last "normal" period at the box office.

&#8220;While we're still working to close the gap with the pre-pandemic marketplace, we're now less than 10% behind 2019 at the same point on the calendar,&#8221; says Rentrak's head of marketplace trends Paul Dergarabedian. &#8220;That's pretty significant, particularly given how much the theatrical marketplace has changed over the past seven years.&#8221;

Holiday offerings like Lionsgate's " The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping ," which is expected to debut above $100 million prior to Thanksgiving, as well as a pair of Christmas tentpoles in Warner Bros. sequel "Dune Part Three" and Disney's Marvel epic " Avengers: Doomsday ," should help to shrink the deficit. Other potential hits on the calendar include Universal's starry comedy &#8220;Focker in Law&#8221; and Sony's action threequel &#8220;Jumanji: Open World.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;With &#8216;Hunger Games,' along with the Thanksgiving corridor and a very important December still ahead, there's a real opportunity to continue building on that momentum,&#8221; Dergarabedian adds.

This year has been defined by unexpected triumphs, including Amazon MGM's space epic &#8220;Project Hail Mary,&#8221; Lionsgate's musical biopic &#8220;Michael,&#8221; A24's psychological thriller &#8220;Backrooms&#8221; and the Focus Features horror film &#8220;Obsession.&#8221; Meanwhile, the anticipated blockbusters like Christopher Nolan's Homer adaptation &#8220;The Odyssey&#8221; and Sony's Marvel adventure &#8220;Spider-Man: Brand New Day&#8221; far exceeded projections, with $1.8 billion and $2.5 billion, respectively. After a record-breaking summer, the fall was comparatively light. However, the season has managed to field some unlikely winners, most notably A24's electric Elizabeth Holmes documentary &#8220;You Can See Everything&#8221; with $89 million domestically to date. Those victories have offset the misses, including Tom Cruise's satire &#8220;Digger,&#8221; Disney's live-action &#8220;Moana&#8221; and Amazon MGM's toy-inspired &#8220;Masters of the Universe.&#8221;

&#8220;It's the movies and the marketing that matter more than anything to general audiences,&#8221; Dergarabedian says. &#8220;And this year has demonstrated what can happen when audiences have an eclectic mix of films to see on the big screen.&#8221;

&#8220; London didn't need another hotel,&#8221; says Nick Jones, as we watch the Thames flow by St. Clement, his latest argument that perhaps it did. &#8220;But I think we've cut through here by doing things differently.&#8221;

It's a familiar claim in luxury hospitality, but Jones has form. In 1995, he opened the original Soho House in the city's creative district, pioneering a shabby-luxury aesthetic that became a blueprint for members' clubs worldwide. Since he stepped down as CEO in 2022, the empire he built has weathered headlines about overcrowding, declining service and the difficulty of securing tables. But whatever its fortunes today, Jones transformed London's social scene once. Can he do it again? blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies Listen to the River! 'Arrow for a Stone Heart' Is a Poetic Hybrid Experience Told Through an Indigenous Lens Movies 'Fatherland' Wins Top Camerimage Prize as 'Wild Horse Nine' Nabs Audience Award

The buzz around St. Clement has been deafening, even before its official September launch. In July, the Rolling Stones hosted an album launch in the penthouse upstairs. A crowd of 100 or so VIPs &#x2014; including Daniel Craig and Sacha Baron Cohen &#x2014; pogoed to &#8220;You Can't Always Get What You Want&#8221; before drinking cocktails on a balcony with a panorama spanning Big Ben to The Shard. There was a drone show.

The Serpentine Summer Party &#x2014; a highlight of London's arts scene calendar every year &#x2014; had an afterparty here. Over London Fashion Week, Burberry pitched up for an official dinner. Paul Mescal and Stanley Tucci have both been spotted hanging out, and the gastronomic grandees have begun their descent too. As I tuck into a plate of freshly made pappardelle with a silky veal rag&#xF9; in Caf&#xE9; Clement ($40), I spot Financial Times critic Jay Rayner &#x2014; irascible scourge of London chefs since the Soho House heyday &#x2014; arching an eyebrow over the menu. Scrutiny will pick up considerably when Lunette, a rooftop restaurant helmed by Florence Knight in her first venture since Sessions Arts Club, opens in the fall. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The determination to be &#x2014; as Jones puts it &#x2014; &#8220;faff-free&#8221; is evident the moment you walk through the door of St. Clement. In the lobby, you're immediately lulled by a hit of scented candle, a bespoke creation by Perfumer H. Massive tomes on Basquiat are piled by a crackling fire. Conspicuously absent is any kind of formal check-in. &#8220;Everything is done in advance on WhatsApp,&#8221; explains relationships director Serena Lightner, previously of Chiltern Firehouse. &#8220;You're welcomed, given your key and brought straight to your room.&#8221; No stiff wait by a mahogany desk; no jet-lagged fumbling for your credit card. Rates range from $934 per night for a standard room to $5,765 for the Central River Loft.

Through a discreet door is Bobbi's Bar, the hotel's naughty corner. Here the exposed concrete and warm, if unexciting, shades of the rooms and corridors give way to dark wood paneling, glowering lamps and conspiratorial nooks and crannies, including a daybed. &#8220;I took out all the things that annoy me about hotels,&#8221; is how Jones describes his approach to St. Clement; this must have included not being able to get a nightcap, because Bobbi's enjoys a rare 3 a.m. license. Rumors of impromptu piano sing-alongs suggest St. Clement has taken some inspiration from the vibiest London opening of last year &#x2014; Guinness pilgrimage spot The Devonshire, over in Soho.

Just down the road is the Corner Shop. Another Jones creation, it's an achingly hip deli-cum-caf&#xE9;-cum-wine bar, with menu items handwritten on brown paper bags and artful arrangements of pomegranates and walnuts next to some tea bags with a price tag of $20. I'd never been to a caf&#xE9; where patrons do the meerkat move you see at parties &#x2014; looking over each other's shoulders in case someone more impressive can be spotted &#x2014; but this is meerkat manor. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;The staff are very relaxed,&#8221; Jones tells me &#x2014; this is true, a waiter welcomes me with a gesture not far from a shoulder rub &#x2014; &#8220;I want [St. Clement] to feel like home for people coming from New York and L.A.&#8221; With a 24-hour health club and spa &#x2014; including a 25-meter pool flowing parallel to the Thames outside &#xAD;&#x2014; due to open next year, there's no doubt people traveling in and out of the city will find it a convenient as well as cool-to-be-seen-at spot. For now, Jones insists there are no plans to expand beyond the neighborhood. &#8220;The great thing is there's only one hotel,&#8221; he says with a grin. &#8220;I can know everyone by name.&#8221;

