You could feel it in the air that night, at the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame induction ceremony, held Saturday at L.A. Peacock Theatre and filmed for a December ABC special. The show's producers would relish, as always, the thought of having younger artists salute their elders, or bringing together some powerhouse teamings, coming up with admirably savvy ideas to make sure most of the honorees got their interesting due, regardless of whether they'd survived to make it to this point or felt it was worth the trip if they had. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Priyanka Chopra Jonas, Russell Crowe Sci-Fi Thriller 'Bluefly' Adds 'Beast' Star Daniel MacPherson (EXCLUSIVE)

To wit: Cynthia Erivo and Travis Barker joining forces as a duo truly no one saw coming for a salute to the ailing Phil Collins. Usher channeling the late Luther Vandross. St. Vincent and an all-star metal crew, including Metallica members, rocking out in honor of the absent Iron Maiden. Sienna Spiro bringing the breeziness for the also-absent Sade. Country-rock upstart Stephen Wilson Jr. honoring the most notorious no-shows of the evening, Oasis with an unexpectedly swampy approach to Britpop revivalism.

And then there was superproducer Rick Rubin , who got a big spotlight as an inductee but was obviously not about to perform, so showrunners came up with a novel pairing in his honor, matching the Red Hot Chili Peppers with&#8230; Rubin's beard. Each of the RHCP members had lifelike facsimiles of Rubin's flowing hirsuteness, with the fake beards glued on well enough to withstand a lot of headbanging, looking like a small army of prowling Zen masters.

That is not to neglect the inductees who were able to salute themselves &#x2014; primarily, the hip-hop wing of the class of 2026: Queen Latifah, MC Lyte and Wu Tang Clan. Billy Idol was also present and accounted for in an climactic segment that saw him joined by one of his presenters, Gwen Stefani, as well as longtime cohort Steve Stevens. And there was a hybrid approach for the Joy Division/New Order performance homage, as members of the Deftones and Chvrches joined the one band representative on hand, Peter Hook to sub in for the band members from whom he is deeply estranged and/or are known to be ill. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

And, to bookend the four-and-a-half-hour proceedings, the Hall booked two acts &#x2014; or three &#x2014; who had no direct tie to this year's proceedings but are always worth a tribute, self-honoring or otherwise: U2 opened the show with two numbers, one old and one hot off the presses. And Aerosmith and Run-DMC closed it with a reprise of the &#8220;Walk This Way&#8221; reunion that just two weeks ago closed an Aerosmith comeback at the Hollywood Bowl.

The U2 kickoff came as a surprise&#8230; or at least a surprise to anyone who hadn't spent much time thinking about how much promotional activity the group was already putting in in the past week to set up the release of their first full new album in nine years, &#8220;Carnaval de Luz.&#8221; The newly re-intact foursome, with drummer Larry Mullen Jr. back in the lineup, performed the new release's &#8220;Silencio&#8221; while flanked by men dressed as policemen holding riot shields, who mostly stood in witness to the fresh track before suddenly and alarmingly running laps around the band. Somewhat soothingly, the intimidating lawmen disappeared as the band segued into the familiar sounds of &#8220;Pride (In the Name of Love).&#8221;

The first honoree, MC Lyte, who got in under the auspices of the Early Influence category, was inducted by Remy Ma, and in her own speech described herself as a &#8220;B-girl from Brooklyn looking for my chance to inspire and motivate a generation of people." She did not perform, at the time, but it turned out her live rapping turn would come later, in tandem with Queen Latifah's.

